# Resolution

The reported direct-part layout was not produced by any supported current
request path: UploadPart and UploadPartCopy store payload exclusively in
`multipart_part_segments`. The PoC manufactured an obsolete pre-segment layout
through test-only metadata rewriting, while Argmin is pre-release and does not
support old storage formats.

Rather than retain a zero sentinel and unreachable compatibility branches,
the obsolete representation has now been removed. `part_okh` is absent from
in-progress and committed part records, the SQLite schema, metadata-command and
storage-RPC encodings, canonical checkpoint state, reclaim records, scavenger
references, and object-read reconstruction. Multipart payloads now have one
representable layout: every part's payload is described by its segment rows.
The incompatible metadata-command, storage-RPC, and canonical-state encoding
versions were advanced so old layouts fail closed.

Tests that previously synthesized direct parts were removed or converted to
create and inspect the segmented layout emitted by production UploadPart paths.
`cargo nextest run -p storage` passes with 2,273 tests.

# Summary
Introduced: the new retained-payload authorization path is incomplete for direct multipart part shard-set records. The fix should add multipart_parts with nonzero part_okh to the retained segment set, with the same stored-size/encryption and placement-epoch fields used by snapshotted_multipart_parts_from_storage.
Full-payload object reads now require a retained payload-read capability. The retained capability is populated from standard object segments and segmented multipart part rows, but it omits direct multipart part payloads represented by ObjectPartRecord.part_okh != [0;16]. Those direct part records are still a valid on-disk/object metadata format: snapshot loading includes them in multipart_parts, and read_core converts them into SegmentPayloadRecord values for reading. Because the retained set does not include those descriptors, prepare_object_payload_read rejects the body as outside the retained snapshot, causing GET Object, ranged/part reads, CopyObject, and UploadPartCopy from such objects to fail with an internal error. This does not bypass authentication, but it can make existing valid multipart object data unavailable after this change.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm retained-payload handoff construction includes standard object segments and segmented multipart part segments but omits nonzero ObjectPartRecord.part_okh direct parts.
- [x] Confirm full multipart snapshots preserve direct object_parts rows while only loading multipart_part_segments for zero-sentinel part_okh rows.
- [x] Confirm read_core converts nonzero part_okh object parts into SegmentPayloadRecord values used for object reads.
- [x] Confirm retained ReadRuntime rejects any body SegmentPayloadRecord not present in the retained set and maps this to InternalError.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce with a real Coordinator GET path using a direct multipart part row, and capture debugger evidence at the rejection point.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC against commit d6f7516679cd72113c90ca3c275a9af23464cdcb. Code review confirmed the retained payload set is populated from snapshot.object_segments and snapshot.multipart_part_segments only, not direct multipart object_parts rows: crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:4383-4429. Snapshot loading for MultipartManifest/FullPayloadLayout reads all object_parts but only loads multipart_part_segments when part.part_okh == [0;16]: crates/storage/src/node/object_read_ops.rs:199-215. The read path still treats nonzero part_okh object parts as direct SegmentPayloadRecord entries: crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read_core.rs:812-824. Retained reads reject any segment absent from the retained set with InternalError: crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:3188-3209. GET Object uses the retained payload runtime: crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read.rs:98-111.

I added PoC test poc_direct_multipart_part_retained_get_should_succeed to crates/server-core/src/coordinator/multipart_tests.rs. It creates a multipart object, rewrites the committed object_parts row to the direct part format with part_okh != [0;16], writes matching shard payload bytes, then calls Coordinator::get_object expecting the valid object to read. Reproduction command failed as expected on the vulnerable commit: cargo test -p server-core poc_direct_multipart_part_retained_get_should_succeed -- --nocapture. Key output: called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: InternalError { reason: "object body is outside its retained payload-read snapshot" }.

Valgrind was attempted but not available in the container; this is also a Rust authorization/availability logic bug, not a memory-safety bug. I then used lldb non-interactively with a breakpoint at runtime.rs:3209. The debugger stopped exactly at the InternalError return and showed the chain prepare_object_payload_read -> ReadHandle::from_multipart -> get_object_on_admitted_route -> get_object -> PoC test, confirming the retained-set mismatch is what makes GET fail.

