# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
No upward adjustment is warranted: this is a real in-scope runtime availability bug validated by code review and an executable unit-test PoC, but it remains bounded to affected legacy/direct multipart objects and requires read authorization or public-read exposure. No evidence supports high/critical impact such as auth bypass, data disclosure, unauthorized write/delete, RCE, or fleet-wide DoS. Medium is appropriate for limited availability loss of valid stored object data.
## Likelihood
high - The HTTP read/copy path is reachable, but practical triggering requires pre-existing legacy/direct multipart metadata or public-readable affected objects. Current multipart upload code appears to create zero-sentinel segmented parts, so a remote attacker cannot generally manufacture the affected state through normal current uploads. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Valid legacy/direct multipart object payloads can become unreadable through GET, range/part reads, CopyObject, and UploadPartCopy. The impact is availability-only and object-format-specific; no confidentiality, integrity, authentication, or privilege boundary is compromised.
## Assumptions
- Static review was limited to repository artifacts in /workspace/argmin; no cloud APIs or deployment inventory were queried.
- The vulnerable path is relevant when argmin-s3 is deployed with a frontend role and reachable S3 HTTP endpoint.
- Triggering the bug requires an existing valid multipart object stored in the legacy/direct part format where object_parts.part_okh is nonzero.
- The attacker can only exercise the failing read/copy path if they are authorized to read the object, or if the bucket/object is intentionally public-read.
- argmin-s3 frontend running the vulnerable commit
- existing multipart object using direct object_parts.part_okh payload coordinates
- attacker has object read permission, copy-source read permission, or the object is public-read
- attacker sends GET Object, ranged/part read, CopyObject, or UploadPartCopy for that object
## Path
Client -> HTTP S3 listener -> authz/read admission -> legacy multipart snapshot -> retained set missing direct part -> read_core direct SegmentPayloadRecord -> retained check fails -> 500/object unavailable
## Path evidence
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:517-618` - Frontend deployments require S3 credentials and default to ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR 127.0.0.1:9000 with optional TLS; this maps the service exposure and secret handling.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:8149-8226` - The main product binds the configured TCP listener and starts the HTTP or HTTPS S3 frontend.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:3620-3633` - HTTP requests are authenticated with authenticate_request; missing auth becomes anonymous, which is later constrained by ACL/public-read authorization.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:2108-2235` - GET Object, Range GET, and partNumber GET are normal HTTP S3 entrypoints that dispatch to coordinator read methods.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:693-715` - Object read authorization requires owner/ACL read or object public-read; this limits reachability to authorized or public-readable objects.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:4383-4429` - The retained payload segment set is built from snapshot.object_segments and snapshot.multipart_part_segments only, omitting direct multipart object_parts with nonzero part_okh.
- `crates/storage/src/node/object_read_ops.rs:199-215` - Full multipart snapshots load all object_parts but only load multipart_part_segments when part.part_okh is the zero sentinel, leaving direct nonzero part_okh parts without segment rows.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read_core.rs:812-824` - The read path still converts nonzero part_okh object parts directly into SegmentPayloadRecord entries that must be accepted by the retained set.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:3188-3209` - Retained reads reject any payload segment absent from the retained snapshot and return InternalError.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/read.rs:98-111` - GET Object now uses retained_read_runtime_for_snapshot, making the incomplete retained set reachable on normal object reads.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/multipart.rs:1493-1502` - Current multipart upload code writes new multipart part records with zero part_okh, which reduces attacker ability to create the affected legacy/direct format through the current public API.
## Narrative
The finding is real and in-scope. The S3 frontend is a normal runtime network surface, and GET Object routes through authorization into a retained read runtime. Static evidence shows the retained set is populated from object_segments and multipart_part_segments, while snapshot loading still includes multipart object_parts and only fetches multipart_part_segments for zero-sentinel part_okh parts. read_core still treats nonzero part_okh object parts as direct payload segments. The runtime then rejects any segment absent from the retained set with InternalError, matching the provided executable PoC result. Severity should remain medium rather than high/critical because there is no authentication bypass, data exposure, write/tamper primitive, or service-wide DoS; impact is limited availability loss for affected legacy/direct multipart objects and requires read authorization or public-read exposure.
## Controls
- AWS SigV4 authentication for authenticated S3 requests
- Anonymous access constrained by bucket/object public-read ACL behavior and PublicAccessBlock settings
- Default frontend bind address is 127.0.0.1:9000 unless ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is changed
- Optional TLS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- Connection and in-flight request limits plus header/body idle timeouts in the HTTP serve layer
- Process refuses to run as root
## Blindspots
- Static-only review did not inventory real deployments or confirm whether ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is internet-facing.
- Static-only review cannot estimate how many production objects use the legacy/direct part_okh format.
- The validation PoC represented legacy metadata by rewriting rows; this supports compatibility reachability but does not prove current public HTTP APIs can create new affected direct-part objects.
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, or cloud load balancer manifests were found or queried in this analysis scope.
